{"ts": 0.259652, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;38;5;208m5.2 — entering and leaving Vim\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;220mvim\u001b[0m is a text editor. Before you \u001b[1;38;5;244medit\u001b[0m anything,\r\nlet's learn how to \u001b[1;38;5;244mleave\u001b[0m.\r\n\r\nFirst, see if Vim is installed:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220mcommand -v vim\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nIf you got a path, Vim is there. If not, install it before continuing.\r\n\r\nA safe entrance:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220mvim --version\u001b[0m     \u001b[2m# prints version info and exits\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nA full Vim session:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220mvim\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThis drops you into Vim with no file. To leave without doing anything,\r\ntype:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220m:q\u001b[0m then Enter\r\n\r\nIf Vim refuses (because you typed something by accident and now there\r\nare unsaved changes), use the \u001b[1;38;5;196mdiscard\u001b[0m form:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220m:q!\u001b[0m then Enter\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;196mYou will need :q!\u001b[0m the first few times you use Vim. Memorize it.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;208m▶ Try \u001b[1;38;5;220mvim --version\u001b[1;38;5;208m and \u001b[1;38;5;220mvim\u001b[1;38;5;208m (then \u001b[1;38;5;220m:q\u001b[1;38;5;208m). Then \u001b[1;38;5;220mnext\u001b[1;38;5;208m.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 1014}
