{"ts": 0.400363, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;38;5;208m5.3 — buffer vs file\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThis is the most important idea in this stage.\r\n\r\nWhen you open a file in Vim, Vim \u001b[1;38;5;244mreads it into a buffer\u001b[0m —\r\na copy in memory. Your keystrokes change the \u001b[1;38;5;244mbuffer\u001b[0m, not\r\nthe file. The file on disk only changes when you \u001b[1;38;5;244mwrite\u001b[0m the\r\nbuffer back.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;244mfile on disk\u001b[0m  →  \u001b[1;38;5;244mbuffer in memory\u001b[0m  →  \u001b[1;38;5;244mfile on disk\u001b[0m\r\n       (read)                  (edit)                     (write)\r\n\r\nThat's why Vim has separate commands for save and quit:\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220m:w\u001b[0m    write the buffer to the file\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220m:q\u001b[0m    quit (only if there are no unsaved changes)\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220m:q!\u001b[0m   quit and discard unsaved changes\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220m:wq\u001b[0m   write, then quit\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;196mPractice this on a file you don't mind editing.\u001b[0m  A\r\n\u001b[38;5;109mpractice.txt\u001b[0m fixture is in this stage's folder. Try:\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220mvim practice.txt\u001b[0m\r\n     type \u001b[1;38;5;220mi\u001b[0m to enter inse", "len": 1024}
{"ts": 0.400462, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "rt mode (vimtutor will teach modes)\r\n     type a few letters\r\n     press \u001b[38;5;208mEsc\u001b[0m\r\n     type \u001b[1;38;5;220m:q!\u001b[0m then Enter\r\n     \u001b[1;38;5;220mcat practice.txt\u001b[0m   \u001b[2m# the file is unchanged\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220mvim practice.txt\u001b[0m\r\n     type \u001b[1;38;5;220mi\u001b[0m, then a few letters, \u001b[38;5;208mEsc\u001b[0m\r\n     type \u001b[1;38;5;220m:wq\u001b[0m, Enter\r\n     \u001b[1;38;5;220mcat practice.txt\u001b[0m   \u001b[2m# now the file has changed\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;208m▶ Try both, then \u001b[1;38;5;220mnext\u001b[1;38;5;208m.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 506}
