{"ts": 1.519048, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;38;5;208m5.4 — calling vimtutor\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;220mvimtutor\u001b[0m is itself a shell command. It launches Vim on a\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;244mcopy\u001b[0m of a tutorial file so you can practice without\r\ndamaging the original.\r\n\r\nCheck whether it's installed:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;220mcommand -v vimtutor\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nIf it prints a path, you're set.\r\n\r\nIf not, you can install it:\r\n  - on Linux: install your distro's full \u001b[1;38;5;244mvim\u001b[0m package (often\r\n    a \u001b[1;38;5;244mvim-enhanced\u001b[0m or \u001b[1;38;5;244mvim\u001b[0m (not \u001b[1;38;5;244mvim-minimal\u001b[0m)\r\n    package).\r\n  - on macOS: Vim ships in macOS, but \u001b[1;38;5;220mvimtutor\u001b[0m may not be\r\n    on PATH. \u001b[1;38;5;220mbrew install vim\u001b[0m via Homebrew adds it.\r\n\r\nThis stage's gate runs through the file-vs-buffer exercise from 5.3\r\nonce more, then you'll be done.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;208m▶ Type \u001b[1;38;5;220mnext\u001b[1;38;5;208m to take Stage 5's gate.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 877}
{"ts": 1.519294, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
